    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you're faced with lesions that have significant morphological irregularities, such as hypertrophic or nodular textures that defy clear definition, it's like trying to navigate a labyrinth with walls that constantly shift and change. The ICD-10 hierarchy is your map, but sometimes the map needs to be drawn by hand, with a quill dipped in the ink of clinical judgment.\n\nIn the case of clinical documentation that describes the texture as \"unusual\" or \"atypical\" without a standard dermatological term, you should first consult the ICD-10 Alphabetical Index, which is like a treasure trove of codes. Look for the closest match to the lesion's description, but remember that the Alphabetical Index is not the final destination; it's merely a waypoint.\n\nIf the Alphabetical Index provides a code that is too general or doesn't fully capture the lesion's unique characteristics, you may need to use a more specific code from the Tabular List. This is akin to finding a hidden passage in the labyrinth that leads you to a more accurate representation of the terrain.\n\nIn situations where the lesion's features are so unique that they don't fit neatly into any existing code, you may need to use a code from the category \"Other specified dermatitis\" (L30",
